1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:01,160
So...

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:03,840
this is Swallowtail.

3
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:09,400
I've got Godot open here, with this little
stretch of coast...

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,340
and I'm going to do most of this from the
terminal.

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,620
I'll move some things around, change a few
values, run the game...

6
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,540
even change stuff while it's running.

7
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:23,900
And Godot stays open the whole time.

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:25,480
So...

9
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:26,300
let me show you.

10
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:31,577
Now, you might be thinking...

11
00:00:32,557 --> 00:00:34,317
why not just do this in the editor?

12
00:00:35,597 --> 00:00:36,837
And for one rock?

13
00:00:37,757 --> 00:00:37,957
Yeah.

14
00:00:38,557 --> 00:00:39,597
Just drag it.

15
00:00:41,837 --> 00:00:43,197
But if I need to do it again...

16
00:00:43,897 --> 00:00:46,317
or do the same thing to fifty rocks...

17
00:00:47,157 --> 00:00:47,917
that's different.

18
00:00:48,877 --> 00:00:53,677
Here I can do it once, save what I did, and
just run it again.

19
00:00:54,637 --> 00:00:59,337
And every command tells me what actually
happened, so I'm not eyeballing

20
00:00:59,397 --> 00:00:59,577
it.

21
00:01:00,677 --> 00:01:00,977
Okay.

22
00:01:01,817 --> 00:01:02,717
Let's get it hooked up.

23
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:06,890
So I've got Godot,

24
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:09,750
and I've got the Swallowtail binary.

25
00:01:10,650 --> 00:01:11,809
I'm in the project folder.

26
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:15,890
Install puts the addon in and turns it on...

27
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:18,590
launch opens the editor...

28
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:20,090
and status...

29
00:01:20,970 --> 00:01:21,230
yep.

30
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:22,450
It can see it.

31
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:26,770
There are a lot of commands.

32
00:01:27,370 --> 00:01:28,410
Over three hundred.

33
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:30,510
I don't remember them either.

34
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:32,410
So I just ask.

35
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:34,630
Node set, help...

36
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:36,810
and it tells me what it wants.

37
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:38,810
Scene tree...

38
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:41,690
that's everything in the scene.

39
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:43,830
And there's the lighthouse.

40
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:48,450
Okay, we're in.

41
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:50,470
Let's change something.

42
00:01:52,363 --> 00:01:53,403
Watch the lighthouse.

43
00:01:54,363 --> 00:01:54,843
First...

44
00:01:55,523 --> 00:01:56,563
where is it right now?

45
00:01:57,663 --> 00:01:58,123
Okay.

46
00:01:58,683 --> 00:01:59,623
That's the position,

47
00:02:00,343 --> 00:02:01,323
that's the scale.

48
00:02:02,243 --> 00:02:03,783
I'm going to move it over here.

49
00:02:07,023 --> 00:02:07,343
There.

50
00:02:08,263 --> 00:02:09,683
And a little bigger.

51
00:02:11,923 --> 00:02:13,583
And that's the actual scene.

52
00:02:14,503 --> 00:02:15,903
If I click it in Godot...

53
00:02:16,783 --> 00:02:17,643
same object.

54
00:02:18,683 --> 00:02:21,003
I can keep going in the inspector if I want.

55
00:02:23,897 --> 00:02:26,236
Now the shore looks a bit empty.

56
00:02:27,257 --> 00:02:28,837
I'll copy one of these rocks...

57
00:02:29,717 --> 00:02:30,517
give it a name...

58
00:02:31,237 --> 00:02:32,077
move it in...

59
00:02:32,617 --> 00:02:33,777
and make it bigger.

60
00:02:37,017 --> 00:02:37,517
That's better.

61
00:02:38,777 --> 00:02:39,457
And the water...

62
00:02:40,577 --> 00:02:41,997
let's turn the waves up.

63
00:02:45,237 --> 00:02:46,497
Yeah, that's rough.

64
00:02:47,557 --> 00:02:47,937
Okay.

65
00:02:48,697 --> 00:02:49,177
Save.

66
00:02:53,897 --> 00:02:56,637
So that's all one command at a time.

67
00:02:57,597 --> 00:02:58,277
And honestly,

68
00:02:59,037 --> 00:03:00,877
I could've done all of that by hand.

69
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:03,697
But say it's not one rock.

70
00:03:04,497 --> 00:03:05,857
Say it's a few hundred.

71
00:03:06,677 --> 00:03:09,497
Or I want five versions of this coast...

72
00:03:10,097 --> 00:03:12,557
calm, stormy, sunset...

73
00:03:13,177 --> 00:03:14,817
and I want to flip between them.

74
00:03:17,057 --> 00:03:18,557
Clicking through that every time?

75
00:03:19,457 --> 00:03:20,037
No thanks.

76
00:03:20,877 --> 00:03:23,537
This is where Swallowtail really shines.

77
00:03:28,897 --> 00:03:30,077
So here's a plan.

78
00:03:30,997 --> 00:03:32,677
It's just a JSON file.

79
00:03:33,577 --> 00:03:34,357
Four steps.

80
00:03:35,237 --> 00:03:36,037
Calm the water,

81
00:03:36,817 --> 00:03:37,997
bring the daylight back,

82
00:03:38,757 --> 00:03:41,217
save, and check the scene's valid.

83
00:03:42,237 --> 00:03:45,697
If that check fails, it stops and tells me
where.

84
00:03:46,737 --> 00:03:47,357
Let's run it.

85
00:03:50,597 --> 00:03:51,497
Water's calm...

86
00:03:52,277 --> 00:03:53,997
and every step came back.

87
00:03:55,057 --> 00:03:55,777
Run it again...

88
00:03:59,017 --> 00:03:59,737
Same result.

89
00:04:00,657 --> 00:04:01,897
That's kind of the point.

90
00:04:02,737 --> 00:04:04,337
I can always get back to this.

91
00:04:05,097 --> 00:04:09,017
And it's a file, so it goes in git with
everything else.

92
00:04:10,257 --> 00:04:11,777
One more thing before we play.

93
00:04:12,657 --> 00:04:13,057
Check...

94
00:04:13,457 --> 00:04:16,797
that runs Godot's own script checker over
the project.

95
00:04:17,757 --> 00:04:18,557
Four scripts...

96
00:04:19,117 --> 00:04:19,717
all good.

97
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:23,030
Okay.

98
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:24,450
Let's play it.

99
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:27,530
I put the rough water back on first...

100
00:04:28,130 --> 00:04:29,610
so there's something to fix.

101
00:04:31,850 --> 00:04:32,670
Start the scene...

102
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:34,510
check the camera...

103
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:36,530
and move forward a bit.

104
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:40,110
Hmm.

105
00:04:40,950 --> 00:04:43,950
From down here, that's a lot of wave.

106
00:04:44,890 --> 00:04:47,630
Let me ask the game what it's actually set
to...

107
00:04:48,570 --> 00:04:49,830
point six five.

108
00:04:52,730 --> 00:04:53,490
Let's drop it.

109
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:55,390
Point one eight...

110
00:04:56,210 --> 00:04:57,110
while it's running.

111
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:01,630
Yeah.

112
00:05:02,070 --> 00:05:02,950
That's more like it.

113
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:04,429
Read it back...

114
00:05:05,310 --> 00:05:06,210
point one eight.

115
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:07,970
Any errors?

116
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:09,470
None.

117
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:12,670
And I'll grab a frame to compare later.

118
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:15,170
Now...

119
00:05:15,450 --> 00:05:16,050
the catch.

120
00:05:16,930 --> 00:05:18,610
That's only in the running game.

121
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:20,890
When I stop, it's gone.

122
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:25,670
So that value has to go into the scene, and
get saved.

123
00:05:26,550 --> 00:05:28,910
Which is a nice little job for an agent.

124
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:33,450
So I'd tell it something like...

125
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:35,410
keep the calm water,

126
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:37,050
lower the sun,

127
00:05:37,870 --> 00:05:41,210
warm the light up, and save it so I can take
a look.

128
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:44,610
And it does the same thing I just did.

129
00:05:45,590 --> 00:05:46,650
Looks up the command...

130
00:05:47,370 --> 00:05:48,210
sets the water...

131
00:05:49,070 --> 00:05:50,050
moves the sun...

132
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:51,470
warms it up...

133
00:05:52,330 --> 00:05:52,870
saves.

134
00:06:03,110 --> 00:06:06,350
Then it reads it all back, so it knows it
worked.

135
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:08,150
It's not guessing.

136
00:06:08,950 --> 00:06:11,270
And I can see every command it ran.

137
00:06:12,170 --> 00:06:12,770
Don't like it?

138
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:15,470
I change it, or ask again.

139
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:18,550
The creative calls are still mine.

140
00:06:21,463 --> 00:06:21,823
Now...

141
00:06:22,363 --> 00:06:22,963
MCP.

142
00:06:23,923 --> 00:06:28,183
If your agent has a terminal, it can just
use these same commands.

143
00:06:28,943 --> 00:06:30,663
You don't need MCP for that.

144
00:06:31,583 --> 00:06:37,463
MCP is for tools like Claude Code, Cursor,
VS Code.

145
00:06:38,303 --> 00:06:41,543
They connect, see the tools, and call them.

146
00:06:43,783 --> 00:06:45,003
Here's one connecting.

147
00:06:45,823 --> 00:06:46,903
It lowers the sun...

148
00:06:49,983 --> 00:06:51,843
...and reads it back.

149
00:06:52,903 --> 00:06:53,623
Same editor.

150
00:06:54,423 --> 00:06:55,863
Same commands underneath.

151
00:06:56,743 --> 00:06:57,663
Nothing's moved.

152
00:07:01,563 --> 00:07:03,103
So, quick recap.

153
00:07:03,923 --> 00:07:05,083
We hooked up the editor...

154
00:07:05,843 --> 00:07:07,103
moved some stuff around...

155
00:07:07,943 --> 00:07:09,183
saved it as a plan...

156
00:07:09,923 --> 00:07:12,203
played the game and fixed it live...

157
00:07:12,783 --> 00:07:15,283
and handed the last bit to an agent.

158
00:07:16,243 --> 00:07:18,003
Use whatever part you want.

159
00:07:18,723 --> 00:07:19,803
Just the terminal...

160
00:07:20,443 --> 00:07:20,943
scripts...

161
00:07:21,543 --> 00:07:22,263
an agent.

162
00:07:23,103 --> 00:07:25,303
The AI part's completely optional.

163
00:07:26,183 --> 00:07:28,863
And it's all still plain Godot files.

164
00:07:29,803 --> 00:07:33,523
Open it without Swallowtail and it's just
your project.

165
00:07:36,430 --> 00:07:36,730
So...

166
00:07:37,470 --> 00:07:38,890
there's a game you want to make.

167
00:07:39,810 --> 00:07:40,850
Start with a scene...

168
00:07:41,410 --> 00:07:42,530
and build from there.

169
00:07:43,770 --> 00:07:44,490
Swallowtail.

170
00:07:45,330 --> 00:07:46,370
At your service.
